<!-- ======================================================== -->
<!-- SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE SUMMARY                             -->
<!-- ======================================================== -->
<h1>1. Executive Summary &amp; Project Overview</h1>
<p>
  In modern organizational administration, traditional disparate systems (such as disconnected spreadsheets, paper leave requests, and isolated payroll sheets) introduce severe inefficiencies, data redundancy, and compliance vulnerabilities. The <strong>Office Management System (OMS)</strong> is a centralized, enterprise web application designed to unify the entirety of an organization's internal workflows into a secure, responsive, and relational software environment.
</p>
<p>
  Developed strictly to meet the rigorous evaluation standards of a University Web Development and Software Design project, OMS delivers a production-grade three-tier architecture utilizing <strong>Python Flask</strong>, <strong>Flask-SQLAlchemy</strong>, <strong>MySQL 8 / MariaDB</strong>, and <strong>Bootstrap 5</strong>. The system establishes a normalized relational foundation containing <strong>68 distinct tables</strong> across 12 operational modules, ensuring complete referential integrity, audit traceability, and zero data loss.
</p>

<h2>1.1 Core Project Objectives</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rigorous Relational Modeling:</strong> Construct a relational schema conforming strictly to Third Normal Form (3NF), containing approximately 68 tables with foreign keys, cascade constraints, and many-to-many association structures.</li>
  <li><strong>Enterprise Soft Deletion:</strong> Implement a system-wide soft-delete paradigm via a reusable SQLAlchemy mixin (<code>AuditSoftDeleteMixin</code>), ensuring no physical record deletion occurs during standard business operations.</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-Tier Access Control:</strong> Enforce Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) across four distinct roles: Super Admin, HR Manager, Department Manager, and Employee, using authenticated session decorators.</li>
  <li><strong>Complete CRUD Capabilities:</strong> Implement Create, Read, Update, and Soft-Delete operations across all business entities with server-side validation, CSRF tokens, and search/filter pagination.</li>
  <li><strong>Automated Payroll &amp; Printable Slip:</strong> Build an automated batch payroll generation engine calculating gross wages, HRA, transport allowances, bonuses, and tax deductions, paired with a formal printable salary slip layout.</li>
  <li><strong>Business Intelligence Hub:</strong> Design an executive reports catalog delivering 47 distinct operational reports with real-time dynamic SQL aggregation, CSV downloads, and styled Microsoft Excel (<code>.xlsx</code>) exports.</li>
</ul>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================== -->
<!-- SECTION 2: ARCHITECTURE                                  -->
<!-- ======================================================== -->
<h1>2. Three-Tier System Architecture &amp; Technology Stack</h1>
<p>
  The system is built upon a standard Three-Tier Enterprise Web Architecture, segregating presentation, application logic, and data storage into distinct, decoupled layers.
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Tier</th>
      <th style="width: 35%;">Component Technologies</th>
      <th style="width: 40%;">Architectural Role &amp; Responsibilities</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Tier 1: Presentation Layer</strong></td>
      <td>
        &bull; HTML5 &amp; CSS3<br>
        &bull; Bootstrap 5.3.3<br>
        &bull; Jinja2 Templates (<code>base.html</code>)<br>
        &bull; Chart.js 4.4 Data Visuals<br>
        &bull; JavaScript UI Interactivity
      </td>
      <td>
        Renders responsive SaaS administration views, interactive modal dialogs, status badges, flash alert notifications, Sprint Kanban boards, and biometric virtual punch terminals. Utilizes template inheritance to eliminate layout duplication.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Tier 2: Business Logic Layer</strong></td>
      <td>
        &bull; Python (v3.11 / v3.15)<br>
        &bull; Flask Framework (v3.1.3)<br>
        &bull; 17 Modular Flask Blueprints<br>
        &bull; Flask-WTF / WTForms<br>
        &bull; Domain Services Layer
      </td>
      <td>
        Enforces business logic, request routing, session authentication, RBAC authorization decorators, server-side data sanitization, CSRF token verification, and domain processing (<code>PayrollEngine</code>, <code>ReportEngine</code>, <code>AuditService</code>).
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Tier 3: Relational Data Layer</strong></td>
      <td>
        &bull; MySQL 8.0 / MariaDB<br>
        &bull; Flask-SQLAlchemy (v3.1.1)<br>
        &bull; SQLAlchemy 2.0 Core<br>
        &bull; PyMySQL Native Driver
      </td>
      <td>
        Persists 68 relational tables, executes indexed queries, enforces foreign key constraints and cascade actions, maintains database session pools, and executes audit log writes.
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">ARCHITECTURAL HIGHLIGHT: MODULAR FLASK BLUEPRINTS</div>
  The Flask application tier avoids monolithic file structures by partitioning route handling across 17 modular Blueprints: <code>auth</code>, <code>dashboard</code>, <code>employees</code>, <code>departments</code>, <code>attendance</code>, <code>leaves</code>, <code>payroll</code>, <code>finance</code>, <code>projects</code>, <code>tasks</code>, <code>clients</code>, <code>assets</code>, <code>documents</code>, <code>communication</code>, <code>reports</code>, <code>recycle_bin</code>, and <code>system</code>.
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================== -->
<!-- SECTION 3: RBAC & CREDENTIALS                            -->
<!-- ======================================================== -->
<h1>3. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Specification</h1>
<p>
  Security in OMS is governed by an explicit Role-Based Access Control matrix. The application segregates organizational authority into four distinct roles, ensuring staff members only access records and operational screens relevant to their official duties.
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 20%;">Role Title</th>
      <th style="width: 45%;">Operational Permissions &amp; Scope of Authority</th>
      <th style="width: 17%;">Default Username</th>
      <th style="width: 18%;">Default Password</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Super Admin</strong></td>
      <td>
        Unrestricted global root authority. Full CRUD across all 68 relational tables. Manages system user accounts, assigns/revokes RBAC roles, edits global configuration settings, reviews security audit trails, and holds exclusive permission to permanently purge records from the Recycle Bin.
      </td>
      <td><code>admin</code></td>
      <td><code>Admin@123</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>HR Manager</strong></td>
      <td>
        Manages the employee lifecycle: staff onboarding, personal profiles, education, prior experience, and documents. Configures departments and job designations. Oversees attendance punch corrections, shift allocations, holiday schedules, leave application reviews/approvals, monthly payroll batches, bonuses, and asset assignments.
      </td>
      <td><code>hrmanager</code></td>
      <td><code>Password@123</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Department Manager</strong></td>
      <td>
        Supervises department workforce: reviews team attendance, manages project deliverables, allocates team members, creates sprint tasks on the Kanban board, updates task progress stages, reviews departmental leave requests, and logs client CRM interactions.
      </td>
      <td><code>deptmanager</code></td>
      <td><code>Password@123</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Employee</strong></td>
      <td>
        Personal self-service access: records daily arrival/departure on the virtual biometric punch terminal, views personal profile data, submits leave requests, monitors leave balance quotas, tracks assigned sprint tasks, inspects personal salary slips, and accesses company announcements.
      </td>
      <td><code>johndoe</code></td>
      <td><code>Password@123</code></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">SERVER-SIDE ENFORCEMENT DECORATORS</div>
  Access boundaries are protected by Python function decorators located in <code>app/utils/decorators.py</code>:
  <code>@login_required</code>, <code>@admin_required</code>, <code>@hr_or_admin_required</code>, and <code>@manager_or_admin_required</code>. Unauthenticated requests are redirected to <code>/auth/login</code>, while unauthorized role attempts trigger an HTTP 403 Forbidden alert.
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================== -->
<!-- SECTION 4: DATABASE ARCHITECTURE                         -->
<!-- ======================================================== -->
<h1>4. Relational Database Design &amp; 68 Normalized Tables Specification</h1>
<p>
  The database schema has been engineered to Third Normal Form (3NF). Every table contains a unique primary key, uses explicit foreign key relationships, avoids repeating groups, and eliminates transitive dependencies. The schema spans <strong>68 relational tables across 12 modules</strong>:
</p>

<h2>Module A: User &amp; Access Management (8 Tables)</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Table Name</th>
      <th style="width: 75%;">Relational Schema &amp; Purpose</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>users</code></td>
      <td>Stores system authentication credentials, usernames, email addresses, password hashes (PBKDF2:SHA256), contact numbers, active flags, and soft-delete audit fields.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>roles</code></td>
      <td>Defines system RBAC roles (Super Admin, HR Manager, Department Manager, Employee) with unique role codes and descriptions.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>permissions</code></td>
      <td>Stores granular operational permission nodes (e.g., <code>employee.create</code>, <code>payroll.run</code>, <code>leaves.approve</code>).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>user_roles</code></td>
      <td>Many-to-Many association table mapping <code>user_id</code> (FK) to <code>role_id</code> (FK) with unique composite constraints.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>role_permissions</code></td>
      <td>Many-to-Many association table mapping <code>role_id</code> (FK) to <code>permission_id</code> (FK).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>user_sessions</code></td>
      <td>Active session ledger storing session tokens, client IP addresses, browser user-agent signatures, and login expiry timestamps.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>login_history</code></td>
      <td>Security audit table logging every login attempt, success/failure status, target username, client IP, and UTC timestamp.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>password_reset_tokens</code></td>
      <td>Stores cryptographically generated, time-limited password recovery tokens linked via FK to <code>users.id</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Module B: Department &amp; Employee Management (12 Tables)</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Table Name</th>
      <th style="width: 75%;">Relational Schema &amp; Purpose</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>departments</code></td>
      <td>Business units with unique department codes, department titles, annual operational budgets, and reporting manager foreign keys.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>department_locations</code></td>
      <td>Physical office premises mapping departments to specific office buildings, floors, and room numbers.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>designations</code></td>
      <td>Corporate job titles (e.g., Software Engineer, HR Lead) with pay scale grades (minimum and maximum salary bands).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>employees</code></td>
      <td>Primary personnel table linking <code>user_id</code>, <code>department_id</code>, <code>designation_id</code>, unique <code>emp_code</code>, joining date, and self-referential reporting <code>manager_id</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>employee_profiles</code></td>
      <td>One-to-One bio-data table storing date of birth, gender, marital status, blood group, nationality, and national ID/passport numbers.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>employee_contacts</code></td>
      <td>Secondary contact directory storing personal mobile numbers, corporate emails, emergency numbers, and LinkedIn/Skype handles.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>employee_addresses</code></td>
      <td>Relational residency table storing current and permanent street addresses, city, state, postal code, and country.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>employee_education</code></td>
      <td>Academic qualifications tracking degree titles, educational institutions, majors, graduation years, and GPA/percentages.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>employee_experience</code></td>
      <td>Prior employment history logging company names, previous designations, service tenures, and key responsibilities.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>employee_documents</code></td>
      <td>Uploaded identity cards, degree certificates, employment contracts, and resumes with server file paths and file sizes.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>employee_skills</code></td>
      <td>Workforce competency matrix tracking technical/professional skills and proficiency ratings (Beginner, Intermediate, Advanced, Expert).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>employee_emergency_contacts</code></td>
      <td>Workplace safety directory storing next-of-kin names, relationships, primary contact numbers, and residential addresses.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Module C: Attendance Management (5 Tables)</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Table Name</th>
      <th style="width: 75%;">Relational Schema &amp; Purpose</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>shifts</code></td>
      <td>Work shift profiles defining shift titles (Morning, Day, Evening), standard start times, end times, and allowable grace minutes.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>shift_assignments</code></td>
      <td>Roster table mapping individual employees to work shifts with effective start and end dates.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>attendance</code></td>
      <td>Daily attendance registry storing date, employee FK, check-in datetime, check-out datetime, calculated work hours, and status (Present, Late, Absent, Half-Day).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>attendance_logs</code></td>
      <td>Raw biometric telemetry records capturing punch timestamp, punch mode (IN/OUT), terminal IP, and verification status.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>holidays</code></td>
      <td>Annual holiday calendar tracking public holidays, company days off, and observed non-working dates.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Module D: Leave Management (5 Tables)</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Table Name</th>
      <th style="width: 75%;">Relational Schema &amp; Purpose</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>leave_types</code></td>
      <td>Leave categories (Annual Paid, Sick, Casual, Maternity, Unpaid) with yearly allocated quotas and paid/unpaid flags.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>leave_balances</code></td>
      <td>Annual leave ledger per employee tracking total allocated days, used days, and remaining available days.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>leave_requests</code></td>
      <td>Employee leave applications storing start date, end date, total days, employee comments, and status (Pending, Approved, Rejected).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>leave_approvals</code></td>
      <td>Managerial signoff audit trail recording reviewing manager FK, review action, decision timestamp, and official remarks.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
