<!-- ======================================================== -->
<!-- SECTION 11: VIVA DEFENSE GUIDE                           -->
<!-- ======================================================== -->
<h1>11. University Viva Defense &amp; Technical Evaluation Guide</h1>
<p>
  Use this Q&amp;A guide when presenting and defending this project before your academic evaluation panel:
</p>

<h3>Q1: Why did you adopt a Three-Tier Architecture instead of a simple two-tier script?</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Answer:</strong> A Three-Tier Architecture separates the Presentation Tier (Bootstrap 5 &amp; Jinja2), Business Logic Tier (Flask Blueprints and Domain Services), and Data Tier (MySQL 8 via SQLAlchemy). This separation guarantees maintainability, modularity, and security. Database queries and business computations are kept out of HTML templates, allowing the database schema or frontend styling to evolve independently without breaking core logic.
</p>

<h3>Q2: How does your database design achieve Third Normal Form (3NF)?</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Answer:</strong> The schema eliminates partial and transitive dependencies by normalizing data across 68 tables. Instead of storing repeated columns (e.g., secondary phone numbers, university degrees, or prior job tenures) as comma-separated strings or nullable fields inside the <code>employees</code> table, dedicated child tables (<code>employee_contacts</code>, <code>employee_education</code>, <code>employee_experience</code>) are linked via Foreign Keys to <code>employees.id</code>. Every non-key attribute depends solely on the primary key.
</p>

<h3>Q3: Why is the Soft-Delete pattern critical in an enterprise application?</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Answer:</strong> In an ERP or office management system, business records are interconnected. For example, an employee is linked to payroll slips, attendance logs, and sprint tasks. Executing a standard SQL <code>DELETE FROM employees</code> either triggers cascading deletions that destroy historical payroll records, or fails due to foreign key constraints. The <code>AuditSoftDeleteMixin</code> sets <code>is_deleted = True</code>, capturing who deleted the record and when. This ensures zero data loss, maintains referential integrity, and allows one-click recovery from the Recycle Bin.
</p>

<h3>Q4: How does the Automated Monthly Payroll Engine calculate net pay?</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Answer:</strong> The <code>PayrollEngine</code> queries active employees and retrieves their assigned salary structure. It calculates gross pay, adds approved discretionary bonuses for that period, calculates and subtracts statutory withholdings (taxes, provident funds, health deductions), and calculates the final Net Payable salary. It commits individual line items to <code>payroll_details</code> and produces a formal salary slip formatted with print CSS styles.
</p>

<h3>Q5: How does your application protect against SQL Injection and CSRF attacks?</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Answer:</strong> SQL Injection is prevented by using SQLAlchemy's Object-Relational Mapping (ORM), which uses parameterized queries. User input is never concatenated directly into SQL statements. Cross-Site Request Forgery is prevented using Flask-WTF, which injects a unique, encrypted CSRF token into every HTML form that must validate on the server before any POST request is processed.
</p>

<h3>Q6: How does the Reports Hub generate both CSV and Excel (.xlsx) files dynamically?</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Answer:</strong> The <code>report_service.py</code> module defines 47 distinct operational report configurations. When a user requests an export, the service runs the corresponding SQLAlchemy query. For CSV, it streams comma-separated rows with proper MIME headers. For Excel, it uses OpenPyXL to build an in-memory workbook with styled navy-blue headers, thin borders, alternating light-gray row fills, and auto-calculated column widths.
</p>

